WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:29.400
And ai just built a strange, beautiful game about the end of everything, and you can play it right now. This is Models &amp; Agents for Beginners, Episode 163. A writer gave an ai named Astra a job that sounded almost impossible. The request was not a short story, it was a whole playable game. The setting was a quiet suburb, something huge and unknowable was arriving. The writer did not want jump scares, not horror, not sadness either.

00:00:29.400 --> 00:00:59.380
They wanted that slow feeling that everything is about to change and nothing can stop it. A prompt is the set of instructions you type to an ai, like a very detailed wish. Here is the wish, almost word for word. Make a game about imminence, something very big and very strange is happening. A suburb and the arrival of a vast, unknowable presence. Do not make horror, invoke the end of all things coming inevitably, but not sad or scary.

00:00:59.380 --> 00:01:28.560
What came back was a short moody game that runs right in a web browser. You do not download an app, you just open it on a phone or a laptop and start clicking. It is called Imminence Afternoon and it was shared on X. Picture asking a classmate who has great stories to invent a tiny world on the spot. Not only the plot, the rooms, the mood, and the choices you can tap. Astra did not stop at a paragraph, it made scenes you can move through.

00:01:28.560 --> 00:01:53.180
That is a big shift from help me write an essay to help me make a little world I can share. Think of a book report versus putting on a school play. One is words on a page, the other is something people walk into and feel. If you like games, art, or storytelling, this is the part that should make your brain light up. You do not need years of coding class to turn a feeling into something playable.

00:01:53.180 --> 00:02:00.660
The same kind of AI many people already use for homework can now help build a tiny creative project in minutes.

00:02:01.240 --> 00:02:11.020
Precise instructions act like a recipe with exact ingredients. Ask for gentle and strange, and the dish comes out gentle and strange, not loud and spooky.

00:02:11.020 --> 00:02:26.460
You can try it yourself, search for Imminence Afternoon and open it in any browser. Click through the scenes slowly. Notice how the tone stays big and odd without turning scary. That match between the wish and the mood is the whole trick.

00:02:27.180 --> 00:02:37.740
Now, how does a typed wish become buttons, scenes, and a feeling you can walk around in? You already know a baby version of this. When you text, your foreign guesses the next word.

00:02:37.740 --> 00:02:45.860
Stretch that guess, not one word, the next beat of a story, the next place, the next choice a player might want.

00:02:46.560 --> 00:02:57.320
First, the system reads your instructions, the way you read a group project brief before anyone starts. Then it breaks the idea into small pieces, like sorting Lego by color before you build.

00:02:57.320 --> 00:03:08.420
Moved in one pile, location in another, feeling, possible actions. For each little pile, it predicts what fits using patterns from millions of stories it has seen.

00:03:09.100 --> 00:03:22.220
None of those scenes were sitting in a folder, pre-written, waiting to be copied. It is making fresh calls the moment you ask, brick by brick. Then it does that again for the next screen and the next choice and the next line of text.

00:03:22.220 --> 00:03:34.240
After every brick, it glances back at the original brief, like a student checking the assignment sheet. That glance is why the whole walkthrough can stay consistent even though nobody planned the map in advance.

00:03:35.000 --> 00:03:45.300
A film director does something similar, making sure every scene still belongs to the same movie. Because the work happens in tiny repeated guesses, the thing can feel alive.

00:03:45.300 --> 00:03:56.500
Nobody had to draw every picture by hand or write every line of dialogue the old way. Try a new prompt tomorrow and the guesses land a little differently, so the world feels personal.

00:03:57.220 --> 00:04:10.200
Once you see it as next word guessing, grown up into next scene guessing, the magic gets a lot less foggy. You can hold that idea in your head now. A wish, then pieces, then guesses, then a place you can click.

00:04:10.200 --> 00:04:23.540
Speaking of things you can actually do in the next five minutes, trip planning is a mess of moving parts, flights, food, and the classic, what if it rains problem, all in the air at once, like juggling.

00:04:24.100 --> 00:04:36.000
A new guide collected 15 ready to copy prompts that turned chat GPT into a travel buddy. Chat GPT is the chatbot a lot of you have already tried for homework and random questions.

00:04:36.000 --> 00:04:46.960
These prompts are written so you can paste them in, no special skill, no coding. You can ask for a day by day plan, budget meals, or backup ideas for bad weather.

00:04:47.560 --> 00:04:58.400
There are also versions for hidden local spots and for rewriting the day when a plan falls apart. That way you stay flexible without starting from a blank page every time something changes.

00:04:58.400 --> 00:05:17.540
Here is your experiment. Open the chat GPT website or the app and start a new chat. Paste this and swap in a city you actually care about. Act as a local guide and create a three day itinerary for your city that mixes free activities with one paid experience under $30 per person.

00:05:18.540 --> 00:05:28.620
Read what comes back like a challenge. Would you really do day two? What would you cut? Then ask it to fix the rainy day version and watch the plan bend instead of breaking.

00:05:29.320 --> 00:05:39.900
Those copy and paste lines work like recipe cards, same steps, different kitchen, still dinner. While you are in a trying mood there is another helper living on phones right now.

00:05:39.900 --> 00:05:49.900
Muse is an AI helper app from meta and it has been climbing the download charts in the United States. It is now one of the most downloaded apps in the country.

00:05:50.540 --> 00:06:00.580
It can chat, answer questions, and help with everyday tasks without leaving your pocket. It is free to download and free to use, which makes it an easy side by side test.

00:06:00.580 --> 00:06:07.940
Different companies AI's have different personalities. The way two teachers can explain the same chapter and feel nothing alike.

00:06:08.700 --> 00:06:17.240
Search your app store for meta AI or Muse. Install it and ask a homework question in the style of a favorite youtuber. Then keep going.

00:06:17.880 --> 00:06:27.560
The app is built to feel like a conversation so follow up questions are the point. Treat it like a classmate who has read a lot and never gets tired of okay but why?

00:06:27.560 --> 00:06:38.480
Notice what feels clearer than chat GPT or fuzzier or just differently weird. That comparison is the experiment. Same question, different pocket genius.

00:06:39.360 --> 00:06:50.780
Zooming out from toys and trips, California just signed new laws about AI chatbots and kids. A chatbot is a program you talk to in text like a conversation that lives on a screen.

00:06:50.780 --> 00:07:03.060
The laws set clearer limits on how those systems can interact with people under 18. The goal is to cut down risk while still letting useful tools exist like age rules on videos and games.

00:07:03.780 --> 00:07:10.880
It is one of the first big state level pushes to treat AI more like other online services that need protections for younger users.

00:07:10.880 --> 00:07:22.600
The rules aim to protect miners and they are not universally supported. The company behind Claude, another AI chatbot, also just put out its biggest report yet on stopping misuse.

00:07:23.380 --> 00:07:31.180
Andthropic published detailed examples of how people tried to use the system for harmful things and how the team caught and blocked those attempts.

00:07:31.180 --> 00:07:45.660
The high level summary is a clearer picture of what AI safety looks like when it is not just a slogan. The report covers attempts involving cyber attacks, influence campaigns, secret watching of people, biology and building weapons.

00:07:46.280 --> 00:07:59.140
It states that every operation described was disrupted. And that's a wrap. If any of today's stories made you go, huh, that's cool. Go play with it. Curiosity is how every expert started. See you tomorrow.

